tests/isla/traces.sh traces the floating-point forms from armv9p4-checked-fp.ir, which this diff
makes from armv9p4-checked.ir, armv9p4.ir with armv9p4-checked.diff applied, where armv9p4.ir is
the Armv9.4-A snapshot of the Sail model (isla-snapshots d8b31014).  The Sail model computes each
floating-point result by rounding a real number, which Isla does not implement, so the traces
leave the floating-point functions abstract with --abstract, and Isla makes the result of an
abstract function a fresh variable, which needs a return type of fixed width (executor.rs 950-971
of Isla e9b5d945).  FPAdd, FPSub, FPMul, FPDiv, FPSqrt, FPRoundInt, and FixedToFP return bits(N)
for the width N of their operands or their argument N, and FPToFixed returns bits(M) for its
argument M.

The diff adds wrappers that take and return vectors of fixed widths and call the original
function: FPAdd64, FPSub64, FPMul64, FPDiv64, and FPSqrt64 of 64-bit operands, FPAdd32, FPSub32,
FPMul32, FPDiv32, FPSqrt32, and FPRoundInt32 of 32-bit operands, FixedToFP64 of a 64-bit integer
with N = 64, FixedToFP64_32 of a 64-bit integer and FixedToFP32_32 of a 32-bit integer with
N = 32, FPToFixed64 of a 64-bit operand with M = 64, and FPToFixed32_64 and FPToFixed32_32 of a
32-bit operand with M = 64 and M = 32.  A dispatcher of each function, FPAdd_sized and the others,
tests the width of its first operand, and for FixedToFP_sized and FPToFixed_sized also N or M,
calls the wrapper of those widths, and calls the original function for every other width.  The
call of each function in the execute function of FADD and FSUB (float_arithmetic_add_sub), FMUL
(float_arithmetic_mul_product), FDIV (float_arithmetic_div), FSQRT (float_arithmetic_unary), the
scalar FRINT forms (float_arithmetic_round_frint), and UCVTF and SCVTF from a general register and
the conversions such as FCVTZU to a general register (float_convert_int) serves the forms of every
element size, and the diff makes it call the dispatcher in place of the function.  An abstract
wrapper therefore stands only for the call of its widths: a form whose widths no wrapper covers,
such as FADD on H registers, still calls the original function, whose arithmetic on real numbers
stops Isla's run with an error.  A dispatcher computes what the original function computes for
every width, so without --abstract the edit changes no trace: the traces of the integer forms and
the memory forms from the edited IR equal those from armv9p4-checked.ir, those of LDR, STR, and
STRB after the fields of each struct are sorted by name, an order that varies between Isla's runs.

The new functions lie before FPSub_ZA, carry no source locations, and their exception paths jump
to `arbitrary`, as the IR's own functions do.  Each call site keeps its statement index, so no
jump target moves.  Apply with
`cp armv9p4-checked.ir armv9p4-checked-fp.ir && patch armv9p4-checked-fp.ir < armv9p4-checked-fp.diff`.

